{"authored" [{"question" "q-80266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/review.json" "file_sha256" "0c1c14ca6e5f716cdb9ad1c1409ee1c2c757a5fa276bf34e6f1fe464ac4b718f" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0f51fb6863ec7b809159c261cd87890fe636a8584879f0790bcfc97d8baec053" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/verification.json" "basis" 1745 "rationale" "Make the source worked solution's explicit containing-region construction part of the study request. D subset R is sufficient for zero-extension integral equality; an equality for one chosen function does not in general require containment because cancellation can occur. q80266 decisively demonstrates the difference through the odd integrand xy: all original rectangles satisfy the literal equality, while only II and III contain D. This lesson's related practice/canonical prompts now consistently state the intended containment criterion. Preserve original source prompts, accepted keys, decisions, grades, unchanged field/answer identities and historical question versions."} {"question" "q-80266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For this question, $R$ must contain every point of $D$. Extending the integrand by zero on $R\\setminus D$ then preserves the integral: the function equals the original integrand throughout $D$ and contributes zero outside it. The rectangles must therefore be checked for containment, as explicitly required in the prompt.\n\nFirst, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle I does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles II and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nThe ellipse has $-1\\le x\\le1$ and $-2\\le y\\le2$. Rectangle I excludes $(0,2)$, so it does not meet the containment requirement. II and III contain the entire bounding box of $D$, hence all of $D$.\n\nThe original unqualified integral equality is a weaker condition. Because $xy$ is odd in $x$ and both $D$ and each displayed $R\\cap D$ are symmetric under $x\\mapsto-x$, all three original rectangles give integrals equal to zero. Thus \"I, II, and III\" is correct for the literal original question. The added containment requirement makes \"II and III only\" correct for this study question." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/review.json" "file_sha256" "0c1c14ca6e5f716cdb9ad1c1409ee1c2c757a5fa276bf34e6f1fe464ac4b718f" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0f51fb6863ec7b809159c261cd87890fe636a8584879f0790bcfc97d8baec053" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/verification.json" "basis" 1745 "rationale" "Make the source worked solution's explicit containing-region construction part of the study request. D subset R is sufficient for zero-extension integral equality; an equality for one chosen function does not in general require containment because cancellation can occur. q80266 decisively demonstrates the difference through the odd integrand xy: all original rectangles satisfy the literal equality, while only II and III contain D. This lesson's related practice/canonical prompts now consistently state the intended containment criterion. Preserve original source prompts, accepted keys, decisions, grades, unchanged field/answer identities and historical question versions."} {"question" "q-80266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/review.json" "file_sha256" "0c1c14ca6e5f716cdb9ad1c1409ee1c2c757a5fa276bf34e6f1fe464ac4b718f" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0f51fb6863ec7b809159c261cd87890fe636a8584879f0790bcfc97d8baec053" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80266/verification.json" "basis" 1745 "rationale" "Make the source worked solution's explicit containing-region construction part of the study request. D subset R is sufficient for zero-extension integral equality; an equality for one chosen function does not in general require containment because cancellation can occur. q80266 decisively demonstrates the difference through the odd integrand xy: all original rectangles satisfy the literal equality, while only II and III contain D. This lesson's related practi